  j_eyebrow:"Join us",j_title:"Join Cal Niu",j_lead:"Choose how you would like to take part. We will get back to you soon.",
  fvol_t:"Volunteering",fvol_s:"Come live and learn with us.",
  fact_t:"Activity registration",fact_s:"Book your place in a workshop or event.",
  f_name:"Full name",f_email:"Email",
  fvol_when:"When would you like to come?",fvol_msg:"Why are you interested?",
  fact_which:"Which activity?",fact_people:"Number of people",f_send:"Send request",
  fact_o1:"Natural building workshop",fact_o2:"Permaculture & garden",fact_o3:"Cultural activity",
  fact_o4:"Open day",fact_o5:"Not sure yet",
  j_contact_t:"Or write to us directly",j_contact_p:"You can reach us by email or through Instagram.",
  hi_eyebrow:"The place · before Cal Niu",
  hi_title:"Sant Iscle i Santa Victòria de Llinars",
  hi_intro:"Cal Niu occupies the former rectory of the church of Sant Iscle i Santa Victòria, in Llinars de l'Aiguadora, the capital of the municipality of Castellar del Riu (Berguedà). A place with more than a thousand years of history in the heart of the Aigua d'Ora valley.",
  hi_t_terr:"The land",
  hi_p_terr1:"Llinars de l'Aiguadora spreads along the left bank of the Aigua d'Ora —also known as aigua de Llinars in its upper course—, a tributary of the Cardener, at around 920 metres of altitude. It is not a compact village but a scattering of farmhouses among woods and meadows.",
  hi_p_terr2:"Historically it belonged to the Solsonès and to the viscounty of Cardona, and it was an independent municipality until 1840, when it became part of Castellar del Riu, of which it is now the capital.",
  hi_b1:"Documented since the 9th century",
  hi_b2:"Repopulation by Count Guifré el Pelós (872–878)",
  hi_b3:"Baroque church of the 17th–18th centuries on Romanesque foundations",
  hi_b4:"Listed heritage (IPAC 3207), Castellar del Riu",
  hi_t_orig:"Medieval origins",
  hi_p_orig1:"The place has been documented since the 9th century. It was one of the key points of the repopulation led by Count Guifré el Pelós in the Lord valley, between the years 872 and 878. In that century, Bishop Galderic of Seu d'Urgell organised the first parish life here and the settlement flourished during the High Middle Ages.",
  hi_p_orig2:"That early organisation gave the church of Llinars rights over new churches in the area —Sisquer, Castelltort, la Selva, Tentellatge, la Valldora, el Cint, la Mora, Travil, Terrers and la Pedra. Some of those rights were usurped by Count Sunifred II of Urgell, and Bishop Guisad II was claiming their restitution around the year 948.",
  hi_t_esg:"The church and the rectory",
  hi_p_esg1:"The present church is in a rural Baroque style, built in the late 17th or early 18th century over the old Romanesque temple. It has a single nave with side chapels, a square presbytery and a square bell tower with round-arched windows. Romanesque funerary slabs recovered from the earlier building were embedded into the south façade.",
  hi_p_esg2:"Attached to the west of the church stands the farmhouse that served as the rectory, with a large round-arched portal. That rectory is precisely the building that today is home to Cal Niu. The complex is listed in the Architectural Heritage Inventory of Catalonia.",
  hi_t_niu:"From rectory to nest",
  hi_p_niu:"After centuries of parish and rural life, this space opens a new chapter. Cal Niu wants to fill the old rectory with life again: a place of gathering, learning and care, faithful to the history of the land around it.",
  hi_src:"Sources: Wikipedia and the Architectural Heritage Inventory of Catalonia (IPAC, record 3207)."
}
};

